<p class="lead">A house is the largest object in any nighttime view of your property, and most of the time it simply disappears after dark. Architectural lighting gives it back its shape. At Spring Landscape Lighting we use carefully placed uplights, soft washes and grazing light to reveal brick, stone, columns, gables and entries, so the home reads as a composed, three-dimensional building instead of a dark mass with a porch light. Good architectural lighting in Spring, TX is not about brightness. It is about choosing which surfaces deserve attention and letting the rest fall quietly into shadow.</p>

<h2>What's included</h2>
<p>Every project starts by looking at the facade from where people actually see it: the street, the driveway and the front walk. From there we plan layers, not a row of identical lights.</p>
<ul>
  <li>A site-specific plan showing fixture locations, beam spreads, wattages and color temperature for each surface.</li>
  <li>Professional low-voltage LED fixtures in brass, copper or high-grade aluminum, sized for the height and texture of each wall or feature.</li>
  <li>Direct-burial wire runs sized for the load and distance, with sealed, waterproof connections.</li>
  <li>A properly sized transformer, zoned so the facade can run on a different schedule from trees or paths.</li>
  <li>Final aiming and brightness balancing after dark, which is where most of the design actually happens.</li>
</ul>

<h2>Techniques we use</h2>
<p>Architectural lighting draws on a short list of techniques used with discipline. You can read more about each in our guide to <a href="/landscape-lighting-techniques/">landscape lighting techniques</a>.</p>
<div class="grid-cards">
  <div class="mini-card"><h3>Uplighting</h3><p>Narrow or medium beams set close to the wall to lift columns, piers and the vertical lines between windows.</p></div>
  <div class="mini-card"><h3>Grazing</h3><p>Fixtures set within 6 to 12 inches of a textured surface so light skims across it, pulling out the depth of brick, stacked stone and mortar joints.</p></div>
  <div class="mini-card"><h3>Washing</h3><p>Wider, softer beams set farther back to give smooth stucco, siding or painted brick an even, calm glow.</p></div>
  <div class="mini-card"><h3>Accent and entry light</h3><p>Small, shielded fixtures for address markers, entry arches, shutters and gables, so the front door reads as the destination.</p></div>
</div>
<p>Most fixtures we specify for facades draw roughly 3 to 8 watts as integrated LEDs, compared with 20 to 50 watts for the halogen lamps many older systems still use. Beam angle matters more than wattage: a 15 to 25 degree beam can carry light up a two-story column, while a 40 to 60 degree beam suits a broad wall section. We generally recommend a warm white around 2700K for red and tan brick and 3000K for gray stone or white-painted surfaces. Our <a href="/tools/color-temperature-guide/">color temperature explorer</a> shows how each option looks on common materials.</p>

<h2>Designing for Spring, TX homes</h2>
<h3>Brick, stone and stucco</h3>
<p>Many homes around Spring combine brick on the lower story with siding or stucco above, plus stone accents at the entry. Each material wants a different treatment. Rough stone rewards grazing; smooth stucco shows every hot spot, so it needs wider beams and more distance.</p>
<h3>Two-story walls and tall gables</h3>
<p>Tall facades are common here, and a single wide floodlight aimed up a 25-foot wall usually produces a bright base and a dim top. We match beam spread to height so light carries evenly to the eaves without spilling into upstairs windows.</p>
<h3>Foundation beds and fast growth</h3>
<p>Boxwoods, hollies and loropetalums in foundation beds can swallow a fixture in a single growing season on the Gulf Coast. We place fixtures with future plant size in mind and leave enough slack in the wire to move them later.</p>
<h3>HOAs, weather and irrigation</h3>
<p>Many Spring-area neighborhoods have deed restrictions or HOA guidelines about exterior changes. Low-voltage landscape lighting is often welcome, but it is worth checking whether your association asks for a submittal. We also keep fixtures out of direct sprinkler spray where possible, use sealed connectors suited to wet soil, and mount the transformer above likely standing water after heavy rain.</p>

<blockquote class="pull">The goal is not to light the whole house. It is to light the parts that make it beautiful.</blockquote>

<h2>Common mistakes we avoid</h2>
<ul>
  <li><strong>Evenly spaced uplights across the foundation.</strong> It flattens the facade. We light features, not intervals.</li>
  <li><strong>Lighting windows.</strong> Light aimed through glass is glare for anyone inside and makes windows look like black holes from the street. We aim at the piers between them.</li>
  <li><strong>Cool, mismatched color.</strong> A 4000K to 5000K fixture on warm brick turns it gray and clinical. Mixing color temperatures across one elevation looks accidental.</li>
  <li><strong>Visible hot spots.</strong> A fixture too close to smooth stucco leaves a bright scallop at the base. Distance and beam choice fix this.</li>
  <li><strong>Overpowering the trees.</strong> If the house is twice as bright as everything else, the landscape disappears. Architectural light should be balanced with <a href="/services/tree-garden-lighting/">tree and garden lighting</a> and the softer layer of <a href="/services/pathway-driveway-lighting/">pathway and driveway lighting</a>.</li>
</ul>

<h2>How it fits the rest of the system</h2>
<p>Facade lighting is usually the anchor of a design, but it works best with a few supporting layers. Many homeowners pair it with <a href="/services/smart-lighting-controls/">smart lighting controls</a> so the facade can stay on until late evening while path lights shut off earlier. If you are still sorting out how many fixtures a home like yours typically needs, the <a href="/tools/fixture-estimator/">fixture estimator</a> gives a planning-level count, and our article on <a href="/landscape-lighting-cost-spring-tx/">landscape lighting cost in Spring, TX</a> explains what moves a quote.</p>

